cmds:
-----
1) lsblk
    Lists all block storage devices (disks and partitions) attached to the EC2 instance.
    NAME         SIZE TYPE MOUNTPOINT
        nvme0n1       10G disk
        ├─nvme0n1p1  8.9G part /
        nvme1n1       20G disk
2) df -kh
    Displays all mounted filesystems along with their disk usage
    Filesystem      Size Used Avail Mounted on
    /dev/nvme0n1p1  9G   3G    6G   /
    /dev/nvme1n1   20G   1G   19G   /var/lib/jenkins
3) sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/dev-name
    Creates an ext4 filesystem on the new EBS volume.
    Why?
        A newly attached EBS volume is just raw block storage. Linux cannot store files on it until a filesystem is created.
        Think of it like buying a new hard drive—you must format it before use.
        Warning: This command erases all existing data on the volume.
4) sudo mount /dev/dev-name /path
    Mounts the EBS volume to a directory.
5) sudo vi /etc/fstab 
    Makes the mount permanent across reboots.   
    (here add an entry to mount ec2 with ebs when its gets rebooted)
    echo 'UUID=d44e9432-7aff-4e4f-afb6-a728b24963b0 /var/lib/jenkins ext4 defaults,nofail 0 2' | sudo tee -a /etc/fstab
6) sudo umount /path
    Unmounts the filesystem.
    After unmounting:
        The EBS volume is no longer accessible through /var/lib/jenkins.
        The directory becomes a normal directory again.
        Use this before detaching an EBS volume from an EC2 instance.
7) sudo mount -a
    Mounts all filesystems listed in /etc/fstab.
    Why use it?
        After editing /etc/fstab, run this command to test the configuration without rebooting.
        If there is no output, the configuration is usually correct.
        If there is an error, fix /etc/fstab before rebooting.